WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.411
Film Courage: At the time you made FORBIDDEN
ZONE after all these years that you think

00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:08.800
about the time that you put into it and making
it, did you consider the original release

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:09.800
a failure?

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.540
Richard Elfman, Filmmaker of The FORBIDDEN
ZONE and FORBIDDEN ZONE 2: Economically, yes.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.439
I mean it bankrupted me and I lost my house.

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:19.550
But artistically I was glad I did it.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.500
Film Courage: At the time that you did it
you felt this way?

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.500
Or later you felt…

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.500
Richard: Completely glad I did it.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.500
Film Courage: Really okay, wow.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.500
Richard: Yes.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.050
Film Courage: And so you are able to talk
a little bit about what happened in terms

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.679
of how it bankrupted you?

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:34.930
Richard: Yes, I had no idea what I was doing.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.400
I didn’t clear the music rights.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.770
The animation cost…(although I had a very
talented animator) it cost like 20 times what

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.940
I thought it was going to cost.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.910
And I was just starting in real estate and
part-time jobs as a bus boy and a cab driver

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:57.930
and I’m forking money in.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.780
It was like some people go to film school.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.780
I just made a film.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.390
Film Courage: Have you always done things
this way, sort of seat-of-your-pants and you

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.470
just jump right in and that’s how you learn
the good and the bad?

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:12.470
Richard: Yes.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.610
Film Courage: Did people try to stop you?

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.290
Richard: Just economic.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.290
Only economic.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:22.290
Film Courage: Yes, that’s scary.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:28.110
Richard: I had to shoot it in starts and stops
as I’d get some money.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.240
I had a little bit of help from friends and
some partners.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.560
Mostly on my shoulders.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:38.350
Film Courage: And then I’m assuming you
took out a second on your house in order to

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.110
fund the movie?

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.280
Richard: Yes.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.690
Well more than that.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:47.600
To finally get it released I had to sign away
all control.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.830
So I only got control of the film fully back
a few years ago…a long story.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.860
But I only got one hundred perfect of FORBIDDEN
ZONE a few years ago.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.730
Film Courage: So…I’m sorry.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:01.730
What was the time span?

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:02.730
15 years?

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:03.730
Richard: 40 years.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:04.730
Film Courage: 40 years.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:05.730
Oh sorry.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:06.730
Richard: The film was done 40 years ago.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.040
Film Courage: Wow!

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.050
Sorry 15 seems like 40 years to me.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.860
Are you thinking about this film every day
for the last 40 years?

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.040
Richard: No, I don’t think about it at all.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.540
I think about the next dinner, the next article.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.720
Film Courage: What wine we’re pairing?

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.750
Richard: Yes.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.300
My brother [Danny Elfman] is writing fantastic
music that I have going through my head.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.270
Film Courage: I know you’ve said music is
very important to you.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.270
Richard: Yes.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.110
I’m blessed with a very rich family life.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.220
Film Courage: Sure.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.850
I’m just hoping to get little nuggets that
we can give to our audience, there are a lot

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.849
of people that watch out stuff that…I’m
not saying they haven’t been as blessed

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:50.720
but they want to know how somebody gets to
this level.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.650
And that is what we do with Film Courage.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:58.581
Richard: Well a positive attitude is important
although I don’t know how you convey this

00:02:58.581 --> 00:03:02.090
to somebody who has a negative attitude to
just tell them the words “Have a positive

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.090
attitude!”

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.270
You’re going to get knocked down.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.600
You’ve got to get back up.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.569
Everybody gets knocked down.

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:12.849
Everybody gets knocked down.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:15.600
You’ve just got to get back up and go back
at it.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.599
Film Courage: So you weren’t really thinking
about acquiring the rights back for all this

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:21.900
time?

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.500
Richard: No.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.200
Someone acquired them, made me a partner,
it’s a long story and I only ended up with

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.720
it recently.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:41.050
Film Courage: How would you say the theatrical
release of FORBIDDEN ZONE (the first one)

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.120
changed your life?

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.950
Richard: Well we were in the New Beverly Theater
now owned by Quentin Tarantino and there was

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.900
an arson threat and we had to leave.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.340
No it didn’t change my life.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.250
Well other than losing my house, that changed
my life.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:06.209
But six months later I had a better house
with some real estate partners and things

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:07.610
worked out.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:14.310
But again I had to pick myself up and go at
it again.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.529
If I could give anyone advice it’s tenacity.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:23.240
It’s if you’re tenacious your odds are
better.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:29.839
The other thing I’ve learned is you get
periodic opportunities and we don’t always

00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:32.809
take advantage of them.

00:04:32.809 --> 00:04:36.669
I had a chip on my shoulder with Hollywood
for a long time.

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:43.740
And I was courted by agents and basically
here’s my card, knock on no door, I don’t

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:47.089
need stars, I’ll do it my way whatever.

00:04:47.089 --> 00:04:51.479
But it was like I can’t even tell you a
few of the names that reached out to me.

00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:57.339
But now I have a mellower view with forces
in the industry.

00:04:57.339 --> 00:05:02.699
Then when I had my media company Buzzine I
got to see a little bit how things work and

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:07.530
how the wheels turn so it kind of took the
chip off of my shoulder.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:13.389
Film Courage: Are you able to share how you
see things work?

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:19.940
Richard: Be aware that opportunities come
up and go for it even if you’re going to

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:20.940
fail.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.879
But go for opportunities when they come up,
every one of them.

00:05:25.879 --> 00:05:30.080
Your biggest regret I’ve read from people
at the end of their lives was not attempting

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.919
to do something.

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:37.560
I don’t know how many times Walt Disney
went bankrupt.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:45.259
Film Courage: It’s a common thing we hear.

00:05:45.259 --> 00:05:49.249
I know an actor gets to a point where they
say “I don’t want to work for free anymore.

00:05:49.249 --> 00:05:50.930
I want to be treated as a professional.”

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.800
And that makes sense.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.229
I would imagine that’s the same for directing
theater or film?

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:02.669
Richard: Well I just directed some theater.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:06.159
When you direct small theater (even if you
fill the seats, you’re still going to loose

00:06:06.159 --> 00:06:08.270
money.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:13.159
Although the interesting thing is my wife
Anastasia is in a theater company (Force of

00:06:13.159 --> 00:06:18.069
Nature Productions) and they put on one of
my plays last year.

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:22.219
And in the play I wrote that there was this
quirky band.

00:06:22.219 --> 00:06:28.999
So for the play we actually put a band together
for the play and now the band is existing

00:06:28.999 --> 00:06:31.460
on its own and they’re in my new film (Mambo
de Monaco).

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:38.289
Film Courage: I think I saw (a video) of them
playing and you were playing drums with them?

00:06:38.289 --> 00:06:39.930
Richard: Yes.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:43.380
But I didn’t make any money from the theater
production.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.199
We filled our 90 seats (whatever).

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:47.589
But you don’t make money from small theater.

00:06:47.589 --> 00:06:49.139
It’s for the love of it.

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:51.517
Well you do art for the love of it and if
make money then that’s a blessing as well.

00:06:51.517 --> 00:06:52.517
But you’re not doing it for art.

00:06:52.517 --> 00:06:54.580
And then if you make money, then that’s
a blessing as well.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:59.249
But you’re doing it for art and that should
be its own satisfaction.

